  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nThe remainder of the day will be breezy and warm, with \nsouth/southwesterly winds around 10-15 mph and gusts up to 25 mph. \nThe mid-level ridge axis has begun pushing eastward with the \noncoming shortwave trough from the Dakotas, with the eastern \nperiphery up against a shortwave lifting across the East Coast. This \npattern will contribute in shifting a low-level ridge of warmer \ntemperatures over much of the forecast area tomorrow, bringing high \ntemperatures into the low 90s and hovering just at/below records for \nseveral sites. Coupled with the warmest temperatures of the forecast \nperiod, a tightening pressure gradient between a developing surface \nlow to the northwest and high pressure to the southeast will \ncontribute to continued breezy weather tomorrow (sustained winds \naround 15-20 mph, with gusts up to 35 mph possible). Similar to \nyesterday, despite the dry antecedent conditions and strong \nwinds, RH values hover around 30-50% across much of the forecast\narea. This continued trend increases confidence for Red Flag \nWarning criteria not being met. However, dry and breezy \nconditions still contributes to fire weather concerns, so focus \nremains on that potential going into the forecast tomorrow.\n\nAs the developing surface low lifts off towards the Great Lakes, a \ncold front will push southeastward towards IA/northern MO. The \nsouthern extent of this cold front will dictate how high \ntemperatures get on Tuesday; appreciable spread in solutions still \nexists in probabilistic guidance for temperatures across northern \nMO/northeastern KS, leading to ongoing uncertainty for daytime \nhighs. South of the boundary, guidance continues to indicate \nbuilding MUCAPE amidst strong shear around 40-50 kts with a \nstrengthening LLJ to the west, increasing confidence in the \npotential for strong/severe storms to develop Tuesday evening. Much \nof this convective potential looks to remain elevated during the \novernight hours, with lapse rates at/around 6.5-7 C/km during \nthis time period. Thus, primary hazards look to be hail and \nsevere winds with any strong storms that are able to develop.\n\nContinued impulses along the periphery of the ridge will keep PoPs \nelevated through midweek as the cold front sags southward. Ongoing \nactivity on Wednesday morning makes determining any continued severe \nthreat murky at best for the afternoon/evening. However, continued \nreturns of warm air/moisture south of the boundary alongside \nmarginal shear will maintain some threat of strong/severe storms for \nthat time period. By Thursday, continued model divergence with the \nprogression of the trough over the western CONUS towards the Great \nLakes maintains uncertainty with the end of precipitation chances \nacross the forecast area. Similar to previous forecasts, though, the \neventual progression of the trough towards the Great Lakes will \nusher in high pressure from the northwest, and cooler temperatures \nwill follow to close out the latter half of the forecast period. \nCurrent low temperatures are forecast to reach the 30s by the end of \nthe time period... Something to monitor (and look forward to) with \nsubsequent forecasts.",
